// Returns AN insured item of the damaged type, not THE damaged one — a policy
// can cover two swords, so the type does not name a unique item. Two damage
// entries of the same type are both answered with the same first match.
//
// That is sound today because the answer is used solely to read payout
// attributes (the reimbursement rate), and the covered case — two damages
// against two swords — insures identical swords, which read the same either
// way. The deductible is charged per damage entry, not per matched item, so
// counting is unaffected too.
//
// Two things would break it, neither yet under test:
//   - Two swords with DIFFERENT attributes (say enchantment 3 and 9). Both
//     damages would take the first sword's rate; the second should take its
//     own. This lookup must then become a pairing that consumes what it
//     matches.
//   - More damage entries of a type than the policy covers, which the spec
//     rejects. Matching cannot detect that while every entry is free to reuse
//     one item; it needs a count of items held against entries named.
// The honest name marks the spot for both.
//
// A damage naming a type the policy does not cover is a spec error case with no
// test yet — the assertion stands in until one arrives, at which point the
// missing case becomes the error branch.
